      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "AIM",
          "content": "We previously reported significant associations between mitochondrial single nucleotide polymorphisms (mtSNPs) and myocardial infarction, atherothrombotic cerebral infarction, metabolic syndrome and type 2 diabetes. Here, we assessed the hypothesis that mtSNPs may confer a risk for atherosclerosis, the most important intermediate phenotype of ischemic cardiovascular events."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "The subjects were 1,536 consecutive autopsy cases (827 men and 709 women). The average age at death was 80 years. The severity of coronary atherosclerosis was semi-quantitatively examined on cut sections. We examined 149 mtSNPs using the PCR-Luminex method, with a success rate of 97%. Phylogenetic tree analysis yielded 36 haplogroups. Multiple logistic regression analysis was performed after adjustments for sex, age, and conventional cardiovascular risk factors."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Among the 45 mtSNPs with minor genotype frequencies >0.05, 6 mtSNPs were associated with coronary atherosclerosis. Among 10 haplogroups with frequencies >0.04, haplogroups A and M7a were significantly associated with coronary atherosclerosis, with odds ratios (95% confidence intervals) of 1.80 (1.09-2.97; p=0.023) and 1.92 (1.23-3.01; p=0.004), respectively. Haplogroup D4a, which was previously reported to be associated with extreme longevity in a Japanese population, was associated with pathological myocardial infarction in men with an odds ratio of 2.05 (1.01-4.14; p=0.046)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "The mitochondrial haplogroups A and M7a confer a significant risk for coronary atherosclerosis in the Japanese. The mitochondrial haplogroup may contribute some genetic risk for coronary heart disease."
        }
      ]},
